div.p:
Best of all for men on earth is never to be born,
nor ever to look upon the rays of the keen sun;
but, once born, to pass the gates of Hades as swiftly as may be.

Blessed is he who has seen those things and so goes beneath the earth;
he knows the end of life,
and he knows its God-given beginning.

Of mortals none is blessed,
nor happy;
for no one yet was born free of grief.

These things one must
bear, which one must pass through by nature;
none of the necessary things is terrible to mortals.

For every wife is worse than her husband,
even if the worst man
should marry the woman of good repute;

ever did her husband good by beauty;
but virtue has done good to many; for every good woman,
who has been welded to her husband, knows how to be prudent.

And at the end they become seers and hymn-singers and physicians
and leaders among men upon the earth;
whence they spring up gods, highest in honours.
